## Server actions are public endpoints

A function marked `"use server"` compiles to a POST endpoint with a stable id.
Anyone can call it directly with curl. It does **not** inherit the authorization
of the page that rendered the form, and the fact that the button is hidden in the
UI protects nothing.

- Every server action begins by establishing identity, from the session, before
  it touches anything. Do it through one helper, `requireUser()` in
  `lib/auth.ts`, which calls `supabase.auth.getUser()`, throws or redirects when
  there is no user, and returns the verified user. One helper means one place to
  get it right, and the harness recognises that name (see `.harness/config.sh`
  for the full list it accepts, and how to add another if this project uses a
  different auth library).
- Every server action validates its arguments with a schema. The arguments arrive
  over the network and can be anything.
- One action file exports only actions. Anything else exported from a
  `"use server"` file also becomes a public endpoint.
- If an action really is meant to be callable by anyone, write
  `// @public-action` with the reason, so the decision is visible in the diff.

## Route handlers

- Public until proven otherwise. Every `route.ts` states its access level and
  enforces it, or carries `// @public-route` with a reason.
- Webhook handlers verify the signature before parsing the body, and they are the
  one case where no session exists.
- Validate `params` and `searchParams`. They come from the URL and are strings of
  the attacker's choosing.

## Middleware is not authorization

Middleware runs at the edge, before your code, and has historically been
bypassable through header manipulation. Use it for session refresh and for
redirecting a signed-out user to the login page, which is a user experience
concern. Never let it be the only thing standing between a request and data.
The enforcing check belongs in the query, backed by RLS.

## The serialization boundary

Anything a server component passes to a client component is serialized into the
HTML payload and is readable in view-source. Rendering only one field of an
object does not help, because the whole object crossed the line.

- Shape the data before passing it down. Pick fields explicitly.
- Never pass a whole database row to a client component.
- Never pass a token, a secret, or an internal identifier you would not print on
  the page.

## Caching

Getting this wrong serves one customer's data to another, which is the worst bug
in the catalogue and one that is nearly invisible in testing.

- Any cache key covering user-specific data includes the user or tenant id.
- `unstable_cache` and `revalidate` are global by default. Per-user data does not
  belong in them unless the key carries identity.
- Mark reviewed caching paths with `// @cache-reviewed` so the harness stops
  asking.
- Prefer `React.cache` for per-request memoization, which does not cross requests.

## Environment variables

- `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is inlined into the browser bundle at build time. It is
  published. The anon key and the publishable Stripe key belong there. Nothing
  else does.
- Server variables read at module scope in a file that could be imported by a
  client component will silently become `undefined`. Use `server-only` to make
  that a build error.
- Validate the environment at boot with a schema, so a missing variable fails on
  deploy rather than on the first request that needs it.

## Errors and loading

- Every route segment that fetches has `loading.tsx` and `error.tsx`. A segment
  without them shows a blank screen when something goes wrong.
- `error.tsx` shows a generic message. Stack traces go to the logs.
- Use `notFound()` rather than a 403 when hiding a resource the user may not see,
  so the response does not confirm that the resource exists.
